<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satricum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satricum was an ancient town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, at Borgo Le Ferriere near Latina south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> and Volsci in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE. Its temple of Mater Matuta yielded the Lapis Satricanus, a Latin inscription of about 500 BCE naming the companions of a Publius Valerius, possibly Publius Valerius Publicola, one of the earliest Latin texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satricum.jpg" alt="Satricum" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satricum_1983.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Satricum stood on the right bank of the Astura River, about 60 km (37 mi) southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in low ground south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> at the northwestern edge of the Pontine Marshes, and was reached from Rome by a road running roughly parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a>. Huts of many shapes show that the acropolis was intensively used in Latial periods II to IV (1000 to 580 BCE). The sanctuary of Mater Matuta on its summit went through three building phases from the late 7th to the early 5th century BCE, in Etrusco-Ionian, Campanian and central-Italic traditions in turn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Satricum was an Alban colony and a member of the Latin League of 499 BCE. The Volsci took it around 488 BCE; a necropolis of the 5th and 4th centuries BCE in the southwest of the city, whose finds show strong Volscian connections, corroborates that tradition. In 386 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> stormed the town, and in 385 BCE they planted a colony of 2,000 settlers there, which Volsci and Latins of Praeneste took in 382 BCE; in 381 BCE four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> marched on Satricum and won a fierce battle there.</p><p>After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Volscian force was routed near Satricum in 377 BCE, the Latins burned the town in revenge, sparing only the temple of Mater Matuta. The Volsci rebuilt it in 348 BCE, but in 346 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> besieged it, took the surrender of 4000 of the enemy and destroyed it, sparing the temple a second time. Afterwards ancient authors mention Satricum only in connection with that temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Inscriptions naming Mater Matuta have settled that the acropolis just south of Le Ferriere is ancient Satricum, which Antonio Nibby had placed at Borgo Montello; whether it was also Suessa Pometia, a city known from legend, as C. M. Stibbe proposed, is still discussed. Excavations began in 1896 under H. Graillot, and since 1977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> institutions have dug there in annual campaigns. An exhibition of about 700 artefacts opened in June 2014 in the former iron mill and strawboard factory at the foot of the acropolis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Satricum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They took over existing Latin towns such as Velitrae, <b>Satricum</b> and Antium, and Anxur (Tarracina) on the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a></li></ul></section>
